TMUX-STATUS

tmux-status.sh is a small, readable shell script that prints a single-line
system status intended for use in the tmux status bar.

It is designed for minimal and traditional Unix environments, favoring
clarity, predictability, and direct access to system interfaces over
frameworks or daemons.


OVERVIEW

tmux-status.sh outputs a compact summary of system information suitable
for inclusion in tmux status-right or status-left.

The script is intentionally simple and avoids background processes,
configuration files, or persistent state.

It aims to be:

- small and easy to read
- easy to audit and modify
- predictable in behavior
- suitable for tmux, minimal window managers, and panel-less setups
- friendly to Slackware and other traditional Linux systems


DISPLAYED INFORMATION

tmux-status.sh displays:

- CPU usage (percentage)
- memory usage (used / total)
- battery status with correct multi-battery handling
- current X11 workspace (when available)
- current date and time


DESIGN PRINCIPLES

tmux-status.sh follows a conservative, Unix-style design.

In particular:

- no daemons or background services
- no caching or state files
- no dependency on desktop environments
- no integration with NetworkManager or systemd components

All information is gathered at runtime using standard system interfaces.


CPU USAGE

CPU usage is obtained using vmstat.

The script computes CPU usage as the inverse of idle time, which provides
a stable and well-understood metric suitable for status displays.

This approach is widely supported across Linux systems.


MEMORY USAGE

Memory usage is obtained using free.

The displayed value represents used memory relative to total memory,
formatted for quick readability in a tmux status line.


BATTERY STATUS

Battery information is read directly from /sys/class/power_supply.

Features:

- correct handling of multiple batteries (for example, ThinkPad systems)
- support for both energy_* and charge_* interfaces
- explicit state indicator:
  - "+" charging
  - "-" discharging
  - "=" fully charged

If no battery is present, the battery field is omitted.


WORKSPACE DETECTION

When running under X11, the current workspace is detected using EWMH
properties via xprop.

Specifically, tmux-status.sh reads:

- _NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP

If X11 is unavailable or the property cannot be read, the workspace field
is omitted.


DATE AND TIME

The date and time are printed using the system date command, formatted
for compact display in tmux.


EXAMPLE OUTPUT

Example output as shown in a tmux status bar:

CPU:12% | MEM:3.1G/7.7G | BAT:78%- | WS:2 | 2025-01-29 21:47


DEPENDENCIES

tmux-status.sh relies only on tools commonly available on a standard
Slackware installation.

Required:

- POSIX shell (/bin/sh)
- coreutils
- procps (for vmstat and free)
- tmux

Optional (for workspace display):

- X11
- xprop

If X11 or xprop is unavailable, workspace information is skipped.


INSTALLATION

tmux-status.sh is a single shell script.

To install locally:

install -m 0755 tmux-status.sh ~/.local/bin/tmux-status.sh

Or system-wide:

install -m 0755 tmux-status.sh /usr/bin/tmux-status.sh


TMUX CONFIGURATION

To use tmux-status.sh in tmux, add the following to your ~/.tmux.conf:

set -g status-right "#(~/.local/bin/tmux-status.sh)"

Or, if installed system-wide:

set -g status-right "#(/usr/bin/tmux-status.sh)"

You may adjust status-right-length if needed:

set -g status-right-length 100


USAGE OUTSIDE TMUX

Although designed for tmux, tmux-status.sh can be run directly from a
terminal:

tmux-status.sh

This can be useful for testing or for use in other status bar tools.


SCOPE AND LIMITATIONS

tmux-status.sh is intentionally minimal.

It does not provide:

- icons or graphical elements
- color output (unless handled by tmux)
- network statistics
- per-core CPU details

The script is intended as a simple building block rather than a complete
system monitor.


